  <Item>
    <Element>StudentPersonal</Element>
    <Description>
      <p>This object contains all the personal information related to the student.</p>
      <p><strong>Considerations around Program Participation</strong></p>
      <p>
        The Unity Data Model supports multiple patterns for representing student program participation. Historically, many implementations have
        modeled program membership using program-specific elements within <code>StudentPersonal</code>. This historic view reflects only the current
        state of participation. Unity now also extends the <code>StudentParticipation</code> object to support more transactional, program-centric
        representations.
      </p>
      <p>
        Existing program-related elements in <code>StudentPersonal</code> remain valid for implementations that rely on them today. However,
        implementations that need clearer program timelines, many-to-one program modeling, or cleaner event flows can also use
        <code>StudentParticipation</code>, either alongside <code>StudentPersonal</code> (Hybrid) or as the primary source of truth (Transactional).
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Traditional (StudentPersonal-centric)</strong>
          <p>
            Best suited to simple systems that want a single authoritative record for a student, with program participation represented directly
            in <code>StudentPersonal</code>. This pattern reflects only the current state of participation and relies on external mechanisms and
            business processes to ensure that data is kept up to date. It works well where a single system owns the record and consumers do not
            require detailed history or timelines.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hybrid</strong>
          <p>
            Best suited to environments that need to support both existing integrations and newer program-centric use cases. In this pattern,
            some or all program participation is modeled in <code>StudentParticipation</code>, while selected program indicators remain in
            <code>StudentPersonal</code> for compatibility and ease of use. It relies on clear rules about which object is authoritative in which
            context and on synchronization mechanisms to keep the two views aligned.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Transactional (StudentParticipation-centric)</strong>
          <p>
            Best suited to systems that treat program membership as a series of transactions over time, using <code>StudentParticipation</code>
            as the primary representation of program entry, exit, and status changes. This pattern supports multiple records per student and
            program, enabling complete histories, timelines, and audit trails while keeping <code>StudentPersonal</code> focused on core identity
            and demographics. It works well where event-driven integrations, reporting, and analytics depend on precise, time-bound program data.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Description>
